    "otherOrganization": "VDMA and the Alliance for OpenUSD",
    "introduction": [
      "OPC 40010-1 describes what a robot is — its topology, its axes, its loads and its operating state — and defines no way to tell it to do anything. A client can read that a robot exists, has six axes and is in Automatic mode, and then has no standard verb with which to move it. Commanding therefore happens below the interface, in vendor motion languages that differ from manufacturer to manufacturer, so a cell that mixes two robots mixes two integrations of work that is identical in both.",
      "The gap is not motion planning, and this document does not try to close that one. Trajectory generation, inverse kinematics, collision avoidance and servo control belong to the robot controller, which does them better than anything reachable over a network could. What is missing is the layer above: a way to say *move there*, *grasp that*, *pick from here and place at that*, to watch the request through a lifecycle that survives a dropped connection, and to be told plainly when the robot refuses.",
      "This document defines that layer. Intents are a DataType hierarchy rather than one Method each, so a single submission and a mission step are the same shape and a new verb is a subtyping act. Each submitted intent is an OPC 10000-10 program instance, so its outcome survives the Session that requested it. Queueing and blending are adopted unchanged from PLCopen and VDA 5050 rather than reinvented. And clause 10 says plainly what this interface is not: it is not safety-rated, it reports what the safety system enforces and refuses what would exceed it, and neither of those makes anything safe."
    ],
    "draftBanner": "PROVISIONAL WORKING DRAFT — not normative, not endorsed by the OPC Foundation. The document number, the Mantis project identifier and every NodeId in this document are placeholders; the OPC Foundation assigns the real values."
  },
